  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 1215 AM CDT Sat Jun 13 2026\n\nA temporary shift in the pattern is expected to bring increased rain \nchances and cooler temperatures for the first part of next week. \nWith an upper ridge building over the eastern Pacific and a broad \nupper trough over the Great Plains, Midwest and Great Lakes, the \nridge over Texas will be suppressed to the south. With this setup a \ncold front will move south into the Big Country by Sunday afternoon, \nand may reach I-20 by evening. The front is forecast to continue \nmoving south Sunday night and should just south of our forecast area \nby 7 AM Monday. The better chance of showers and thunderstorms will \nbe along and behind this front as it progresses south. With a brief \nperiod of northwest flow aloft, an embedded disturbance is progged \nto move southeast across our area late Sunday night into Monday. \nThis could bring additional showers and storms, with the higher rain \nchances shifting into our southern counties Monday afternoon. A \nrather moist airmass is progged with precipitable water values \nmostly in the range of 1.6 to 2.1 inches Sunday into Monday. This \nwill be supportive of heavy rainfall, and slow-moving or training \nconvection would bring a potential for flooding. Temperatures will \nbe cooler on Monday behind the front with considerable cloud cover. \nHighs Monday are expected to be limited to the upper 70s to lower \n80s. A few lingering showers and storms will be possible into \nTuesday morning for the far southeastern part of our area.\n\nThe hottest temperatures so far this season will be possible \nWednesday into late week, when a ridge aloft over the southwestern \nstates is progged to shift east across Texas. Highs are forecast to \nbe 100-105 degrees Wednesday in portions of the Big Country and \nConcho Valley. Forecast highs Thursday generally range from 100-103 \nin most of the southeast half of our area, to 104-106 across the \nnorthwest half. With relatively humid conditions expected, heat \nindices will be pushing 105-110 on Thursday afternoon. Anticipate \nthat this will bring the need for heat headlines (Heat \nAdvisory/Excessive Heat Warning). The details in the forecast \nThursday night and Friday become more uncertain, however. Some of \nthe medium range models are bringing a disturbance north into Texas \nfrom eastern Mexico, and having the disturbance stall over central \nTexas and bring a low chance (20-30 percent) of showers and \nthunderstorms to our area.",
